      "id": "exam/edexcel-9fm0-01-2021-november/q2",
      "question": "<ol class=\"parts\"><li>Use the Maclaurin series expansion for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\cos x\"></span> to determine the series expansion of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\cos^2\\left(\\dfrac x3\\right)\"></span> in ascending powers of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\"></span>, up to and including the term in <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x^4\"></span><br>Give each term in simplest form. <span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></li><li>Use the answer to part (a) and calculus to find an approximation, to 5 decimal places, for<div class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\frac1x\\cos^2\\left(\\frac x3\\right)\\right)\\mathrm dx\" data-display=\"true\"></div><span class=\"marks\">(3)</span></li><li>Use the integration function on your calculator to evaluate<div class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\frac1x\\cos^2\\left(\\frac x3\\right)\\right)\\mathrm dx\" data-display=\"true\"></div>Give your answer to 5 decimal places. <span class=\"marks\">(1)</span></li><li>Assuming that the calculator answer in part (c) is accurate to 5 decimal places, comment on the accuracy of the approximation found in part (b). <span class=\"marks\">(1)</span></li></ol>",
